            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Why Version APIs?</h2><p>Versioning allows you to evolve your API without breaking existing clients. Add new features, change behavior, or deprecate endpoints while maintaining backward compatibility.</p></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Setup</h2><p>Use the ASP.NET API versioning package to read versions from URL segments, headers, or query strings and to expose available versions to clients.</p><pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet add package Asp.Versioning.Mvc</code></pre><pre><code class="language-csharp">builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =>
{
    options.DefaultApiVersion = new ApiVersion(1, 0);
    options.AssumeDefaultVersionWhenUnspecified = true;
    options.ReportApiVersions = true;
    options.ApiVersionReader = ApiVersionReader.Combine(
        new UrlSegmentApiVersionReader(),
        new HeaderApiVersionReader("X-Api-Version"),
        new QueryStringApiVersionReader("api-version"));
}).AddApiExplorer(options =>
{
    options.GroupNameFormat = "'v'VVV";
    options.SubstituteApiVersionInUrl = true;
});</code></pre></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>URL Versioning</h2><p>URL versioning embeds the version in the path which is easy for clients to consume and for routing to resolve; keep controllers separate per version when responses differ significantly.</p><pre><code class="language-csharp">[ApiController]
[Route("api/v{version:apiVersion}/[controller]")]
[ApiVersion("1.0")]
public class ProductsV1Controller : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IEnumerable&lt;ProductV1Dto&gt; Get() => // V1 response
}

[ApiController]
[Route("api/v{version:apiVersion}/[controller]")]
[ApiVersion("2.0")]
public class ProductsV2Controller : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IEnumerable&lt;ProductV2Dto&gt; Get() => // V2 response with new fields
}</code></pre></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Deprecating Versions</h2><p>Mark old versions as deprecated so clients have a migration window; communicate removal timelines in API docs and release notes.</p><pre><code class="language-csharp">[ApiVersion("1.0", Deprecated = true)]
[ApiController]
public class ProductsV1Controller : ControllerBase { }</code></pre></section>
                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Version your API from day one.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>URL versioning (/v1/, /v2/) is most common.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Mark old versions as deprecated before removal.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
